1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,399
Cent #6129.

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:05,839
A penny struck in 1979,

3
00:00:05,919 --> 00:00:08,720
made to carry one image: Lincoln.

4
00:00:09,199 --> 00:00:09,919
Look for him.

5
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:12,400
The edge of the profile survives.

6
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:14,480
The features have worn away.

7
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:17,519
The Trait Council calls this Faceless.

8
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:20,559
85 of the 10,000 Cents carry it.

9
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:22,480
Where Liberty was struck,

10
00:00:22,559 --> 00:00:23,760
something has settled over

11
00:00:23,839 --> 00:00:26,320
the letters: Obscure Adhesive.

12
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:29,920
Around it, obsidian, verdigris and gold.

13
00:00:30,079 --> 00:00:31,800
The mark as index.

14
00:00:32,079 --> 00:00:34,479
"The official icon (Lincoln's head)

15
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:37,840
fades; what matters are the unplanned indices.

16
00:00:38,079 --> 00:00:39,359
The penny shifts from being

17
00:00:39,439 --> 00:00:41,520
an image to being a time-stamp.

18
00:00:41,679 --> 00:00:44,320
Its patina says: I have been around."

19
00:00:44,799 --> 00:00:47,920
Only seven Cents are both Faceless and gold.

20
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:50,520
A coin collector would call this damage.

21
00:00:50,719 --> 00:00:52,640
In CENTS it runs the other way.

22
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:54,719
"The more imperfect the coin,

23
00:00:54,759 --> 00:00:56,560
the more history you can see,

24
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:57,920
the more value we give it."

25
00:00:58,320 --> 00:00:59,759
One cent, by law.

26
00:01:00,079 --> 00:01:03,039
What it is worth beyond that is still being decided.
